        void draw(int x) { }
    }
}

/var/tmp $ RUN_LOCALE="-Duser.language=en -Duser.country=US"
/var/tmp $ java $RUN_LOCALE -jar checkstyle-14.3.0-all.jar -c config.xml Test.java
Starting audit...
[ERROR] /var/tmp/Test.java:7:24: 'x' hides a field. [HiddenField]
[ERROR] /var/tmp/Test.java:12:29: 'x' hides a field. [HiddenField]
Audit done.
Checkstyle ends with 2 errors.
```

---

**Describe what you expect in detail.**

I expect no violations. A nested enum is implicitly static (JLS 8.9), and so is a class declared inside an interface (JLS 9.5), so the instance field `x` of `Test` cannot be used in `paint` or `resize`; `javac` reports "non-static variable x cannot be referenced from a static context" if they try. The check already skips this case for an explicitly `static` nested class (`Line`) and, since #14028, for a nested record (`Point`), which is also implicitly static.

In `HiddenFieldCheck.visitOtherTokens`, a new frame is static only when the type has a `static` modifier or is a `RECORD_DEF`. A nested `ENUM_DEF` is not treated as static, and a type whose parent is an interface body is not either, so `FieldFrame.containsInstanceField` keeps looking in the outer class.
