### Issue Checklist

- [x] I understand that issues are for reporting problems and requesting features, not for off-topic comments, and I will provide as much detail as possible to help resolve the issue.
- [x] I have checked the pinned issues and searched through the existing [open issues](https://github.com/CherryHQ/cherry-studio/issues), [closed issues](https://github.com/CherryHQ/cherry-studio/issues?q=is%3Aissue%20state%3Aclosed), and [discussions](https://github.com/CherryHQ/cherry-studio/discussions) and did not find a similar suggestion.
- [x] I have provided a short and descriptive title so that developers can quickly understand the issue when browsing the issue list, rather than vague titles like "A suggestion" or "Stuck."
- [x] The latest version of Cherry Studio does not include the feature I am suggesting.

### Platform

macOS

### Version

v2.1.3

### Is your feature request related to an existing issue?

At present, Model Providers in CherryStudio are mainly managed in a list. When the number of configured providers increases, it becomes difficult to organize and quickly locate them by project, purpose, vendor, or environment. The current management interface also lacks the following capabilities:

- Organizing providers into folders;
- Using an icon/grid view to improve information density and browsing efficiency;
- Categorizing and filtering providers with tags;
- Manually rating and sorting providers;
- Manually pinning frequently used providers;
- Importing or exporting an individual Model Provider.

As a result, managing, finding, backing up, and reusing providers becomes inconvenient when many providers are configured. This is especially noticeable when migrating a single provider between different devices or CherryStudio instances. Users may need to export all provider configurations just to migrate one provider, which increases the effort and may affect existing configurations.

### Desired Solution

I would like CherryStudio’s Model Provider management page to provide more flexible organization, browsing, filtering, and migration capabilities.

Specifically, I would like to see support for:

- **Folder management**: Allow users to create, rename, and delete folders, and move Model Providers between folders;
- **Icon/grid view**: Provide an icon view similar to a file manager in addition to the list view, improving information density and browsing efficiency;
- **Tag management**: Allow users to assign multiple custom tags to a provider and filter providers by tags;
- **Search and combined filtering**: Support filtering by a combination of folders, tags, enabled status, ratings, and other properties, as well as searching provider names, model names, and tags;
- **Manual ratings**: Allow users to rate providers using a 1–5 star rating or a custom score, and sort providers by rating;
- **Manual pinning**: Allow users to pin frequently used providers and display them first in both list and icon views;
- **Individual provider import/export**: Allow users to import or export a single Model Provider without having to process all provider configurations. The exported data could include the provider’s basic configuration, model list, folder, tags, icon, rating, and pinning status;
- Secure handling of sensitive information: API keys and other sensitive information should be excluded or masked by default during export, and requested again from the user after import;
- **Import conflict handling**: When importing a provider with the same name, provide options such as overwrite, create a duplicate, skip, or merge.

Ideally, users should be able to switch between list and icon views, organize providers using folders and tags, quickly locate providers through search and multi-condition filters, and rearrange folders or ordering through drag-and-drop. An action menu for each provider could provide direct “Import” and “Export” actions for individual providers.

These features could be implemented incrementally. For example, folder management, icon/grid view, and individual provider import/export could be introduced first, followed by tags, ratings, pinning, and more advanced combined filtering.

I do not currently have a specific UI mockup, but the overall interaction could draw inspiration from the following products:

- **macOS Finder**: For folder management, list view, icon view, drag-and-drop interactions, and file organization;
- **Notion database pages**: For property fields, tags, multi-condition filtering, sorting, and switching between different views;
- **Obsidian Bases**: For property-based data management, filtering, sorting, and flexible view configuration.


### Alternative Solutions

_No response_

### Additional Information

As the number of objects to be managed increases, a simple list view and search function are usually no longer sufficient. Users typically need several complementary management methods:

- Folder-based organization: To organize objects according to projects, purposes, environments, or other hierarchical relationships;
- Tag-based organization: To categorize objects from different perspectives and support cross-category filtering;
- Metadata management: To record object status and user preferences, such as whether an item is pinned or favorited, its manual rating, enabled status, and other custom properties.

