body_text:
`isValidJWT` decodes the header segment with `atob(header)`, but JWT segments are base64url (RFC 7515). `atob` only accepts the standard alphabet, so it throws on `-` and `_`, the `catch` returns `false`, and a valid token fails with "Invalid JWT". Whether a token hits this depends only on what its header encodes to: a `?` or `>` anywhere in a header value produces one of those characters, so a `jku` URL with a query string is enough.

```ts
import * as z from "zod";

const b64url = (o: object) => Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(o)).toString("base64url");
const header = b64url({ alg: "RS256", jku: "https://idp.example.com/jwks?tenant=abc" });
const token = `${header}.${b64url({ sub: "1" })}.sig`;

z.jwt().safeParse(token).success; // false
atob(header); // InvalidCharacterError
```

Reproduced on 4.6.5 and on `main` (10dda3a). v3's `isValidJWT` maps base64url back to base64 before calling `atob`, and `util.base64urlToUint8Array` does the same for the base64url codecs; the v4 check skips that step. Out of 20,000 generated tokens with random `kid` values, 4.6.5 rejected exactly the 1,871 whose header contained `-` or `_`, and none of the others.

PR creation is limited to collaborators, so the fix is on a branch: https://github.com/colinhacks/zod/compare/main...Prafyl:zod:fix/jwt-base64url-header

It maps the header back to padded base64 the same way `util.base64urlToUint8Array` does. The compiled path calls the same `isValidJWT`, so it needs no change, and the new test fails before the fix in both the `zod` and `compile-mode` projects. Costs about 40 B gzipped on `schemas.ts`; `atob` is forgiving about missing padding, so dropping the padding line halves that if you'd rather not carry it.

