`core/images/archive.ImportIndex` (used by `ctr images import`, the transfer-service import in `core/transfer/archive`, and `client.Import`) synthesises a Docker schema2 manifest for tarballs that carry a `manifest.json` but no `oci-layout`. When it derives each layer's media type from the blob bytes, every compressed layer is labelled **gzip**, even when the blob is zstd:

- `resolveLayers` — the `else` branch after the `Uncompressed` check hardcodes gzip:
  https://github.com/containerd/containerd/blob/f6132dbe1f482cbe0aebc4bd3d8d7a184fb4a2aa/core/images/archive/importer.go#L306-L327
- `detectLayerMediaType` (used when the blob already exists in the store with an uncompressed label, e.g. on a second import) — the `default:` case of `switch compression.DetectCompression(bytes)` is gzip:
  https://github.com/containerd/containerd/blob/f6132dbe1f482cbe0aebc4bd3d8d7a184fb4a2aa/core/images/archive/importer.go#L397-L420

Both branches predate zstd support. containerd already knows zstd in this position: `compression.DetectCompression` returns `compression.Zstd` (https://github.com/containerd/containerd/blob/f6132dbe1f482cbe0aebc4bd3d8d7a184fb4a2aa/pkg/archive/compression/compression.go#L44-L52), and `images.MediaTypeDockerSchema2LayerZstd` was added in #9859 for exactly this Docker-manifest shape (https://github.com/containerd/containerd/blob/f6132dbe1f482cbe0aebc4bd3d8d7a184fb4a2aa/core/images/mediatypes.go#L37, handled in `DiffCompression` and `IsLayerType`). The importer was never updated to use it.

The imported image therefore has a manifest that says `application/vnd.docker.image.rootfs.diff.tar.gzip` for blobs whose first bytes are the zstd frame magic (`28 b5 2f fd`). Unpack still works because the applier autodetects compression, so this is silent until the manifest leaves the node: `ctr images push` publishes a manifest with wrong layer media types, and clients that trust the media type (for example go-containerregistry's `remote` layer, which gunzips based on `MediaType`) fail with `gzip: invalid header`.

A real-world producer of such tarballs is `crane pull` (default `--format=legacy` writes layer blobs compressed as-is, without `oci-layout`) on any image with zstd layers; hand-built `docker save`-style tarballs with pre-compressed layers hit the same code.

### Steps to reproduce the issue

Using the CLI:

```sh
crane pull --format=legacy <image with zstd layers> /tmp/zstd-legacy.tar
ctr -n test images import /tmp/zstd-legacy.tar
# read back the synthesised manifest
ctr -n test content get <manifest digest from 'ctr -n test images ls'> | jq '.layers[].mediaType'
# -> "application/vnd.docker.image.rootfs.diff.tar.gzip" for every layer
ctr -n test content get <one layer digest> | head -c4 | xxd
# -> 28b5 2ffd  (zstd frame magic)
```

Minimal Go reproducer, no daemon or root required. Drop it into `core/images/archive/` (the package currently has no tests) and run `go test ./core/images/archive/ -run TestImportLayerMediaType -v`. The `uncompressed` and `gzip` cases pass; the `zstd` case fails on both the first import (`resolveLayers`) and the second import into the same store (`detectLayerMediaType`):

```go
package archive

import (
	"archive/tar"
	"bytes"
	"context"
	"encoding/json"
	"testing"

	"github.com/containerd/containerd/v2/core/content"
	"github.com/containerd/containerd/v2/core/images"
	"github.com/containerd/containerd/v2/pkg/archive/compression"
	"github.com/containerd/containerd/v2/plugins/content/local"
	"github.com/opencontainers/go-digest"
	ocispec "github.com/opencontainers/image-spec/specs-go/v1"
)

// buildDockerArchive returns a Docker v1.2 tarball (manifest.json, no oci-layout)
// with a single one-file layer compressed with c.
func buildDockerArchive(t *testing.T, c compression.Compression) *bytes.Buffer {
	var layerTar bytes.Buffer
	tw := tar.NewWriter(&layerTar)
	tw.WriteHeader(&tar.Header{Name: "hello", Mode: 0644, Size: 5})
	tw.Write([]byte("hello"))
	tw.Close()
	diffID := digest.FromBytes(layerTar.Bytes())

	var layerBlob bytes.Buffer
	if c == compression.Uncompressed {
		layerBlob.Write(layerTar.Bytes())
	} else {
		zw, err := compression.CompressStream(&layerBlob, c)
		if err != nil {
			t.Fatal(err)
		}
		zw.Write(layerTar.Bytes())
		zw.Close()
	}

	cfg, _ := json.Marshal(ocispec.Image{
		Platform: ocispec.Platform{Architecture: "amd64", OS: "linux"},
		RootFS:   ocispec.RootFS{Type: "layers", DiffIDs: []digest.Digest{diffID}},
	})
	mfst, _ := json.Marshal([]struct {
		Config   string
		RepoTags []string
		Layers   []string
	}{{Config: "config.json", RepoTags: []string{"example.com/repro:latest"}, Layers: []string{"layer.tar"}}})

	var archiveBuf bytes.Buffer
	aw := tar.NewWriter(&archiveBuf)
	for _, f := range []struct {
		name string
		data []byte
	}{{"config.json", cfg}, {"layer.tar", layerBlob.Bytes()}, {"manifest.json", mfst}} {
		aw.WriteHeader(&tar.Header{Name: f.name, Mode: 0644, Size: int64(len(f.data))})
		aw.Write(f.data)
	}
	aw.Close()
	return &archiveBuf
}

func importedLayerMediaType(t *testing.T, store content.Store, buf *bytes.Buffer) string {
	ctx := context.Background()
	idxDesc, err := ImportIndex(ctx, store, buf)
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	var idx ocispec.Index
	b, _ := content.ReadBlob(ctx, store, idxDesc)
	json.Unmarshal(b, &idx)
	var m ocispec.Manifest
	b, _ = content.ReadBlob(ctx, store, idx.Manifests[0])
	json.Unmarshal(b, &m)
	return m.Layers[0].MediaType
}

func TestImportLayerMediaType(t *testing.T) {
	for _, tc := range []struct {
		name string
		c    compression.Compression
		want string
	}{
		{"uncompressed", compression.Uncompressed, images.MediaTypeDockerSchema2Layer},
		{"gzip", compression.Gzip, images.MediaTypeDockerSchema2LayerGzip},
		{"zstd", compression.Zstd, images.MediaTypeDockerSchema2LayerZstd},
	} {
		t.Run(tc.name, func(t *testing.T) {
			store, err := local.NewStore(t.TempDir())
			if err != nil {
				t.Fatal(err)
			}
			if got := importedLayerMediaType(t, store, buildDockerArchive(t, tc.c)); got != tc.want {
				t.Errorf("first import (resolveLayers): media type = %q, want %q", got, tc.want)
			}
			// Importing again into the same store hits detectLayerMediaType.
			if got := importedLayerMediaType(t, store, buildDockerArchive(t, tc.c)); got != tc.want {
				t.Errorf("second import (detectLayerMediaType): media type = %q, want %q", got, tc.want)
			}
		})
	}
}
```

Output on `main` @ f6132dbe:

```
--- FAIL: TestImportLayerMediaType (0.17s)
    --- PASS: TestImportLayerMediaType/uncompressed (0.07s)
    --- PASS: TestImportLayerMediaType/gzip (0.05s)
    --- FAIL: TestImportLayerMediaType/zstd (0.05s)
        first import (resolveLayers): media type = "application/vnd.docker.image.rootfs.diff.tar.gzip", want "application/vnd.docker.image.rootfs.diff.tar.zstd"
        second import (detectLayerMediaType): media type = "application/vnd.docker.image.rootfs.diff.tar.gzip", want "application/vnd.docker.image.rootfs.diff.tar.zstd"
```

### Describe the results you received and expected

**Received:** zstd layer blobs are recorded in the synthesised manifest as `application/vnd.docker.image.rootfs.diff.tar.gzip`.

**Expected:** the media type reflects the detected compression — `application/vnd.docker.image.rootfs.diff.tar.zstd` (the constant containerd already defines for Docker-manifest zstd layers) — or an explicit error for compressions that have no Docker layer media type, rather than silently claiming gzip.

### Proposed fix

Add a small helper in `core/images/archive/importer.go`:

```go
func layerMediaType(c compression.Compression) (string, error) {
	switch c {
	case compression.Uncompressed:
		return images.MediaTypeDockerSchema2Layer, nil
	case compression.Gzip:
		return images.MediaTypeDockerSchema2LayerGzip, nil
	case compression.Zstd:
		return images.MediaTypeDockerSchema2LayerZstd, nil
	default:
		return "", fmt.Errorf("unsupported layer compression %v: %w", c, errdefs.ErrNotImplemented)
	}
}
```

and use it in both `resolveLayers` (replacing the `else` branch, via `s.GetCompression()`) and `detectLayerMediaType` (replacing the `default:` branch). The `--compress` path is unchanged (it still gzips uncompressed layers and labels them gzip). The test above can land alongside as the package's first test.

For comparison, moby's containerd-backed `docker load` (`daemon/containerd/image_import.go`, `saveArchive`) already preserves zstd on import and stores such layers with a zstd media type rather than gzip (it uses the OCI `application/vnd.oci.image.layer.v1.tar+zstd` type). Happy to switch the proposal to the OCI type inside the Docker manifest if maintainers prefer; the Docker constant seemed the consistent choice given #9859.

### What version of containerd are you using?

`main` @ f6132dbe1f482cbe0aebc4bd3d8d7a184fb4a2aa. The same code is on `release/2.3` and `release/2.2` (identical line numbers). `release/1.7` has the same branches at `images/archive/importer.go` L320/L325/L417 but does not have the `MediaTypeDockerSchema2LayerZstd` constant.

### Any other relevant information

The OCI-layout branch of `ImportIndex` is unaffected (media types come from `index.json`), so BuildKit `type=docker` output and `nerdctl save` tarballs are fine; only `manifest.json`-only archives with pre-compressed layers hit this. Not related to #13662 (unpack-side handling of mislabeled OCI uncompressed layers) or #9864 (compression of the archive stream itself).

