body_text:
Replacing keys and then iterating can leave a value unfreed after the map is dropped. The example below reproduces this in a single thread, with or without calling `clear()`.

Tested on Debian 13, Linux x86_64, Rust 1.99.0, at Crossbeam commit `099d0469efcfacb32527bd9451a4fb62586ae537`.

## Reproduction

`Cargo.toml`:

```toml
[package]
name = "skipmap-drop-leak-repro"
version = "0.0.0"
edition = "2021"
publish = false

[dependencies]
crossbeam-skiplist = { git = "https://github.com/crossbeam-rs/crossbeam", rev = "099d0469efcfacb32527bd9451a4fb62586ae537" }
crossbeam-epoch = { git = "https://github.com/crossbeam-rs/crossbeam", rev = "099d0469efcfacb32527bd9451a4fb62586ae537" }

[workspace]
```

`src/main.rs`:

```rust
use std::sync::{
    atomic::{AtomicUsize, Ordering},
    Arc,
};

struct Value {
    id: usize,
    drops: Arc<Vec<AtomicUsize>>,
}

impl Drop for Value {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        assert_eq!(self.drops[self.id].fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed), 0);
    }
}

fn main() {
    let clear = std::env::args().any(|arg| arg == "--clear");
    let cleanup = std::env::args().any(|arg| arg == "--cleanup");
    let keys = [0_u64, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1];
    let drops = Arc::new(
        (0..keys.len())
            .map(|_| AtomicUsize::new(0))
            .collect::<Vec<_>>(),
    );
    let map = crossbeam_skiplist::SkipMap::new();

    for (id, key) in keys.into_iter().enumerate() {
        drop(map.insert(
            key,
            Value {
                id,
                drops: drops.clone(),
            },
        ));
    }

    let _: Vec<_> = map.iter().map(|entry| *entry.key()).collect();

    if clear {
        map.clear();
    }
    if cleanup {
        drop(map.lower_bound(std::ops::Bound::<&u64>::Unbounded));
    }
    drop(map);

    for _ in 0..65_536 {
        let guard = crossbeam_epoch::pin();
        guard.flush();
    }

    let missing: Vec<_> = drops
        .iter()
        .enumerate()
        .filter_map(|(id, count)| (count.load(Ordering::Relaxed) == 0).then_some(id))
        .collect();
    println!("clear={clear} cleanup={cleanup} missing={missing:?}");
    assert!(missing.is_empty());
}
```

Run each command in a separate process:

```sh
cargo run --release --
cargo run --release -- --clear
cargo run --release -- --clear --cleanup
```

Observed output:

```text
clear=false cleanup=false missing=[6]  # assertion fails
clear=true cleanup=false missing=[6]   # assertion fails
clear=true cleanup=true missing=[]     # succeeds
```

Expected: all nine values are eventually dropped. The example reports insertion 6 as missing after dropping the map and repeatedly pinning/flushing. Calling `clear()` followed by an additional search before dropping the map makes the example pass.

## Cause

Iteration unlinks the replaced node at level 0, but the node remains linked at level 1 with a reference count of 1. [`SkipList::drop()`](https://github.com/crossbeam-rs/crossbeam/blob/099d0469efcfacb32527bd9451a4fb62586ae537/crossbeam-skiplist/src/base.rs#L1413-L1437) only walks level 0, so it misses the node. The remaining reference also prevents the node from being deferred to the epoch collector.

## Regression

With Rust 1.77.2, the same nine-insertion sequence passes on `2128578c992a2415d34b1845494d74e0e40e8722` and fails on its immediate successor, `e7b5922ec9da940d2630099c5d6b4877d2463de4` (#1101). This holds both with and without `clear()`. That commit changes the replacement insertion order; it does not change `Drop` or `clear()`.

Related reports: #540 and #1178. The revision tested here already includes the range-iteration fix in #1217.

