It would be extremely useful if the `deno bundle` CLI command could accept the content of the entrypoint via stdin, instead of requiring a physical file on disk.

Similarly, it would be great if the programmatic API (`Deno.bundle()`) could accept an in-memory / string entrypoint.

This would enable bundling scripts with dynamic input behavior, such as on-the-fly code generation, user-supplied scripts and build pipelines that avoid temporary files.

`esbuild` already supports this pattern via its Node API using stdin, including full bundling and module resolution:

https://esbuild.github.io/api/#stdin

Example from esbuild:

```
await esbuild.build({
  stdin: {
    contents: `
      import { add } from "./math";
      console.log(add(2, 3));
    `,
    resolveDir: process.cwd(),
    sourcefile: "virtual-entry.ts",
    loader: "ts",
  },
  bundle: true,
  outfile: "out.js",
  platform: "node",
});
```

This approach has proven very flexible and ergonomic in practice.

