Version: Deno 2.5.6

For instance, for the command list `a; b; c` a Unix shell will perform the following sequence:

```c
pid_t pid = fork();
if(pid == 0) {
    execlp("a", "a");
}
waitpid(pid, NULL, 0);

pid = fork();
if(pid == 0) {
    execlp("b", "b");
}
waitpid(pid, NULL, 0);

execlp("c", "c");
```

While Deno performs:

```c
pid_t pid = fork();
if(pid == 0) {
    execlp("a", "a");
}
waitpid(pid, NULL, 0);

pid = fork();
if(pid == 0) {
    execlp("b", "b");
}
waitpid(pid, NULL, 0);

pid = fork();  // Extra fork/exec/waitpid cycle
if(pid == 0) {
    execlp("c", "c");
}
waitpid(pid, NULL, 0);

exit(0);
```

This means that the parent Deno process uselessly hangs around (and affecting signal delivery). For services started using systemd this means that systemd will consider the wrong process to be the main process affecting, for instance, service readiness notifications.

