> Note: Heavily AI assisted investigation/repro

## Version

```
deno 2.9.6 (stable, release, x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu)
v8 15.0.245.2-rusty
typescript 6.0.3
```

Also seen on 2.9.5. `@grpc/grpc-js` 1.14.4.

## What happens

When an HTTP/2 client session runs a few requests and then sits idle for several minutes, and the server then sends GOAWAY, the whole Deno process dies.

The same script gives two outcomes. Sometimes it aborts on an nghttp2 assertion and exits 134:

```
deno: nghttp2/lib/nghttp2_session.c:2402: int find_stream_on_goaway_func(void *, void *): Assertion `stream->closed_next == NULL' failed.
Aborted (core dumped)
```

Other times it exits 139 and prints nothing at all.

The session `goaway` event never fires, so this happens while nghttp2 is still processing the frame, before Deno reaches JavaScript. No user code can catch it.

## Reproduction

`package.json`:

```json
{
  "dependencies": {
    "@grpc/grpc-js": "1.14.4"
  }
}
```

`deno.json`:

```json
{
  "nodeModulesDir": "auto"
}
```

`repro.ts`:

```ts
import * as grpc from "@grpc/grpc-js";
import http2 from "node:http2";

const CALLS = 13;
const LAST_STREAM_ID = 225;
const IDLE_MS = 250_000;

const server = http2.createServer();
let closed = 0;

server.on("session", (session) => {
  session.on("error", () => {});
  session.on("stream", (stream) => {
    stream.on("error", () => {});
    stream.respond({
      ":status": 200,
      "content-type": "application/grpc",
      "grpc-status": "0",
    }, { endStream: true });
    stream.on("close", () => {
      if (++closed === CALLS) {
        setTimeout(() => {
          console.log(`server: GOAWAY errorCode=0 lastStreamID=${LAST_STREAM_ID}`);
          session.goaway(http2.constants.NGHTTP2_NO_ERROR, LAST_STREAM_ID);
        }, IDLE_MS);
      }
    });
  });
});

server.listen(0, async () => {
  const address = server.address();
  const port = typeof address === "object" && address ? address.port : 0;
  const client = new grpc.Client(`127.0.0.1:${port}`, grpc.credentials.createInsecure());
  const passthrough = (b: Buffer) => b;

  for (let i = 0; i < CALLS; i++) {
    await new Promise<void>((resolve) => {
      client.makeUnaryRequest(
        "/repro.Repro/Ping",
        passthrough,
        passthrough,
        Buffer.alloc(0),
        () => resolve(),
      );
    });
  }
  console.log(`client: ${CALLS} calls done, staying idle for ${IDLE_MS / 1000}s`);

  setTimeout(() => {
    console.log("no crash");
    Deno.exit(0);
  }, IDLE_MS + 5000);
});
```

```
deno install
deno run -A repro.ts
```

Expected: it prints `no crash` and exits 0.
Actual: it dies as soon as the GOAWAY is written, with 134 or 139.

Conditions:

* Idle values of 10, 30, 60 and 120 seconds all finish cleanly. 180 and 250 seconds both reproduce it.
* We only reproduce this on x86_64 Linux, in a container. The same script on macOS arm64 finishes cleanly.

## The GOAWAY this replays

`cloudtasks.googleapis.com` retires an idle connection after about four minutes. Captured on the host that reproduces the crash, using a plain `node:http2` client with no gRPC:

```
t+13.6s  13 requests done, idling
t+253.6s GOAWAY errorCode=0 lastStreamID=225
t+253.6s session close
t+413.6s NO CRASH
```

That client shuts down cleanly. Only the `@grpc/grpc-js` client dies, so the trigger involves what grpc-js does on the callback path.

Every Google Cloud client library talks gRPC through `@grpc/grpc-js`, which uses `node:http2`, so any Deno service that calls a Google API and then goes quiet can hit this, and it takes down the whole process.

## Where the assertion comes from

`session_on_goaway_received` calls `session_close_stream_on_goaway`, which uses `nghttp2_map_each(find_stream_on_goaway_func)` to collect the streams it has to close. The collection borrows `stream->closed_next` as list storage and asserts that the field is free:

```c
/* We are collecting streams to close because we cannot call
   nghttp2_session_close_stream() inside nghttp2_map_each().
   Reuse closed_next member.. bad choice? */
assert(stream->closed_next == NULL);
```

`find_stream_on_goaway_func` is the only place that sets `closed_next` on a stream that is not yet flagged `NGHTTP2_STREAM_FLAG_CLOSED`. So the assertion can only fail if a second collection starts while an earlier one is still live. The window is the close loop that runs right after the collection:

```c
stream = arg.head;
while (stream) {
  next_stream = stream->closed_next;
  stream->closed_next = NULL;
  rv = nghttp2_session_close_stream(session, stream->stream_id, NGHTTP2_REFUSED_STREAM);
  /* stream may be deleted here */
  stream = next_stream;
```

`nghttp2_session_close_stream` invokes `on_stream_close_callback`, which in Deno reaches JavaScript. While that callback runs, every stream still queued in `arg.head` has `closed_next` set and no `NGHTTP2_STREAM_FLAG_CLOSED`. If anything on that path feeds more bytes into `nghttp2_session_mem_recv`, or flushes a queued GOAWAY, then `session_close_stream_on_goaway` runs a second time over the same map and hits the assertion. The same reentry would explain the 139 runs, where the list is walked after a stream has already been freed.

nghttp2/nghttp2#1637 is an older report of a crash in this same function. It was closed as stale with no root cause, and it describes a segfault as well as the assertion.

## Workaround

For Google client libraries, `google-gax` can skip gRPC:

```ts
new CloudTasksClient({ fallback: true });
```

That path goes through `fetch` and never loads `node:http2`. We confirmed it survives the same idle window on the host that reproduces the crash.

