body_text:
I (with Opus 5 and Fable 5.1) found this while working on gracefully shutting down our application. I didn't wanted to do a drive by pull request, because fixing this could bring implications down the line, that I am not aware of as an "outsider".

## Summary

On Deno 2.9.x, calling `Deno.HttpServer.shutdown()` makes every **in-flight HTTP/1.1 request body unreadable at once**. Any read of the body — `request.json()`, `request.body.getReader().read()`, or a `new Request(request, { body })` wrapper — rejects immediately with

```
BadResource: Cannot read body as underlying resource unavailable
```

whether the read was started before or after the `shutdown()` call. On Deno 2.8.3 the same body stays readable and graceful shutdown waits for the handler as documented.

## Environment

| | Result |
|---|---|
| Deno 2.9.4, ubuntu-latest x86_64 (CI, `denoland/setup-deno@v2`) | body read fails |
| Deno 2.9.6, macOS arm64 | body read fails |
| Deno 2.8.3, macOS arm64 | body stays readable |

## Minimal reproduction

```ts
const encoder = new TextEncoder();

const trickle = new ReadableStream<Uint8Array>({
  start: (controller) => {
    controller.enqueue(encoder.encode('{"value":"'));
    const timer = setInterval(() => {
      try {
        controller.enqueue(encoder.encode(" "));
      } catch {
        clearInterval(timer);
      }
    }, 250);
  },
});

const server = Deno.serve(
  { port: 0, hostname: "127.0.0.1", onListen: () => {} },
  async (request) => {
    const body = request.json();
    body.catch(() => {});

    server.shutdown();

    const stillReading = new Promise<string>((resolve) =>
      setTimeout(() => resolve("body still being read after 1500 ms (expected)"), 1500)
    );

    const outcome = await Promise.race([
      body.then(() => "body parsed", (error: unknown) => `body read failed: ${error}`),
      stillReading,
    ]);

    return new Response(outcome);
  },
);

const response = await fetch(`http://127.0.0.1:${server.addr.port}/`, {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "content-type": "application/json" },
  body: trickle,
  duplex: "half",
} as RequestInit);

console.log(`${Deno.version.deno}: ${await response.text()}`);
Deno.exit(0);
```

Output:

```
2.8.3: body still being read after 1500 ms (expected)
2.9.6: body read failed: BadResource: Cannot read body as underlying resource unavailable
```

The order does not matter: starting `request.json()` *after* `server.shutdown()` fails the same way, and so do `request.body.getReader().read()` (message `Cannot read request body as underlying resource unavailable`) and reading through a wrapped `new Request(request, { body: request.body.pipeThrough(new TransformStream()), duplex: "half" })`.

## Where it comes from

Deno 2.9 serves HTTP/1.1 through the new Deno-owned path added in #34446 (`ext/http/http_next.rs`, `serve_http11_raw`, backed by `libs/http_h1`). The error text is produced in `RawH1RequestBodyRead::poll` / `RawH1RequestBodyReadByob::poll` (`ext/http/http_next.rs`, around line 1600 at tag v2.9.4), which returns `BadResource` when the body was cancelled or when `conn.poll_peer_closed(cx)` reports the peer as closed. The only callers of the body's `cancel()` are those two peer-closed branches, so graceful shutdown is tripping the peer-closed detection (`libs/http_h1/conn.rs`, `poll_peer_closed_with`, which treats a zero-length read as EOF) while the client is still connected and still sending. Exactly which step of `op_http_close(graceful = true)` causes that read to return zero bytes was not traced further.

Deno 2.8.3, which still serves HTTP/1.1 through hyper, does not exhibit this.

## Workaround

Use `v2.8.x`, the last release before the Deno-owned HTTP/1.1 path.

