body_text:
Deno’s `node:http` server can stall a persistent connection when a handler sends a response without reading the request body. The first response arrives, but the next request on that connection never reaches the handler before the reproducer’s timeout.

The same code works under Node.js. Calling `req.resume()` before responding also avoids the failure under Deno.

Disclosure: This issue was found and reported by GPT-6 Astra in Codex.

Reproduced with:

- Deno 2.9.6 — macOS, arm64.
- Deno 2.9.2 — Linux, x86_64.
- Node.js 26.8.2 — macOS, arm64: passes.

```js
// repro.mjs
import { createServer } from "node:http";
import { createConnection } from "node:net";
import { once } from "node:events";
import { Buffer } from "node:buffer";
import process from "node:process";

// Run with Node or Deno. --drain enables the application-level workaround.
const drain = process.argv.includes("--drain");
const received = [];
const server = createServer((req, res) => {
  received.push(req.url);
  if (drain) req.resume();
  res.writeHead(req.url === "/reject" ? 404 : 200, { "Content-Length": "2" });
  res.end("OK");
});

server.listen(0, "127.0.0.1");
await once(server, "listening");
const socket = createConnection(server.address().port, "127.0.0.1");
const statuses = [];
let buffer = Buffer.alloc(0);
let timer;

try {
  await once(socket, "connect");
  await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    timer = setTimeout(() => reject(new Error("Second request stalled on the same TCP connection")), 2000);
    socket.on("error", reject);
    socket.on("end", () => reject(new Error("Server closed the connection")));
    socket.on("data", (chunk) => {
      buffer = Buffer.concat([buffer, chunk]);
      const headerEnd = buffer.indexOf("\r\n\r\n");
      if (headerEnd < 0 || buffer.length < headerEnd + 6) return;
      statuses.push(Number(buffer.toString("ascii", 0, headerEnd).split(" ")[1]));
      buffer = buffer.subarray(headerEnd + 6);
      if (statuses.length === 1) {
        socket.write("GET /next HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: localhost\r\n\r\n");
      } else {
        resolve();
      }
    });
    const body = Buffer.alloc(1_000_000, "x");
    socket.write(Buffer.concat([
      Buffer.from(`POST /reject HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: localhost\r\nContent-Length: ${body.length}\r\n\r\n`),
      body,
    ]));
  });
  console.log("PASS", JSON.stringify({ drain, statuses, received }));
} catch (error) {
  console.error("FAIL", error.message, JSON.stringify({ drain, statuses, received }));
  process.exitCode = 1;
} finally {
  clearTimeout(timer);
  socket.destroy();
  server.closeAllConnections();
  await new Promise((resolve) => server.close(resolve));
}
```

Run:

```sh
node repro.mjs
deno run --allow-net=127.0.0.1 repro.mjs
deno run --allow-net=127.0.0.1 repro.mjs --drain
```

**Expected:** the server discards the unread body and handles the next request, matching Node:

```text
PASS {"drain":false,"statuses":[404,200],"received":["/reject","/next"]}
```

**Actual under Deno:**

```text
FAIL Second request stalled on the same TCP connection {"drain":false,"statuses":[404],"received":["/reject"]}
```

With `--drain`, Deno returns both responses successfully. On macOS, the baseline failed in all three repeated Deno runs; Node and the Deno workaround passed all three.

The likely cause is a difference in `parserOnBody()`. [Deno sets `stream._consuming = true`](https://github.com/denoland/deno/blob/v2.9.6/ext/node/polyfills/_http_common.js#L182) when body bytes arrive, even if the application never reads them. [Node’s corresponding implementation does not](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v26.8.2/lib/_http_common.js#L128).

Both implementations use `_consuming` to decide whether response completion should automatically discard the request body. Deno’s assignment can therefore suppress that cleanup. Once the unread buffer fills and socket reading pauses, the next request cannot progress.

[Node’s cleanup code](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v26.8.2/lib/_http_server.js#L1214) explicitly documents automatic draining for requests the application never consumed. Node also has a relevant regression test: [`test-http-dump-req-when-res-ends.js`](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v26.8.2/test/parallel/test-http-dump-req-when-res-ends.js).

