# WebTransport streams are neither finished on close() nor released until garbage collection

**Deno 2.9.6, Linux x86_64.** Reproduction below (needs a cert: `openssl req -x509 -newkey ec -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:prime256v1 -nodes -keyout /tmp/wt-key.pem -out /tmp/wt-cert.pem -days 10 -subj "/CN=localhost" -addext "subjectAltName=IP:127.0.0.1"`).

Two related problems with `WebTransportSendStream`:

1. `writer.close()` (also `writable.close()` and `pipeTo`) does not send a QUIC FIN. The peer never sees end-of-stream; a peer reading to EOF hangs until the idle timeout. Verified against a Rust (quinn/wtransport) peer in both directions and Deno↔Deno.
2. `writer.abort()` does not reset the stream either, and the underlying QUIC stream is only released when the JS object is garbage-collected — `writableStreamForRid(rid, /* autoClose */ false, …)` in `ext/web/webtransport.js` relies on the inner stream's finalizer. Because nothing returns stream credit until GC, a session hits the 100-stream default and `createBidirectionalStream()` throws `RangeError: Connection has reached the maximum number of concurrent outgoing bidirectional streams` after 100 calls (or the peer's opens block). Forcing `gc()` on both peers makes the streams flow again, which confirms the cause.

```ts
// deno run --unstable-net --allow-net --allow-read repro.ts
const cert = await Deno.readTextFile("/tmp/wt-cert.pem");
const key = await Deno.readTextFile("/tmp/wt-key.pem");
const endpoint = new Deno.QuicEndpoint({ hostname: "127.0.0.1", port: 0 });
const listener = endpoint.listen({ cert, key, alpnProtocols: ["h3"] });
(async () => {
  for await (const incoming of listener) {
    const wt = await Deno.upgradeWebTransport(await incoming.accept());
    (async () => {
      for await (const stream of wt.incomingBidirectionalStreams) {
        (async () => {
          const reader = stream.readable.getReader();
          await reader.read(); // reading to EOF would never finish: the client's close() sends no FIN (problem 1)
          reader.releaseLock();
          const w = stream.writable.getWriter();
          await w.write(new Uint8Array([1]));
          await w.close();
        })().catch(() => {});
      }
    })();
  }
})();

const der = Uint8Array.from(atob(cert.replace(/-----[^-]+-----|\s/g, "")), (c) => c.charCodeAt(0));
const hash = new Uint8Array(await crypto.subtle.digest("SHA-256", der));
const client = new WebTransport(`https://127.0.0.1:${endpoint.addr.port}/x`, {
  serverCertificateHashes: [{ algorithm: "sha-256", value: hash }],
});
await client.ready;
for (let i = 0; i < 120; i++) {
  const stream = await client.createBidirectionalStream(); // throws RangeError at i == 100 (problem 2)
  const w = stream.writable.getWriter();
  await w.write(new Uint8Array([1]));
  await w.close();
  const r = stream.readable.getReader();
  await r.read();
  r.releaseLock();
}
console.log("120 streams OK");
```

Expected: `close()` finishes the QUIC stream (FIN), `abort()` resets it, and either releases it immediately so credit returns; 120 streams complete.
Actual: no FIN/RESET is ever sent, and the loop fails at the 101st stream.

