Version: Deno 2.9.7 (also 2.9.3), Linux x86_64 (kernel 7.0)

If a directory is deleted and recreated, and a `node:fs.watch` watcher on it is closed and a new one opened on the same path in the same tick, the new watcher never receives events.

```js
import fs from "node:fs";
import os from "node:os";
import path from "node:path";

const dir = path.join(fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), "w-")), "d");
fs.mkdirSync(dir);

const old = fs.watch(dir, () => {});
await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 50));

fs.rmSync(dir, { recursive: true });
fs.mkdirSync(dir);
old.close();
const events = [];
const fresh = fs.watch(dir, (type, name) => events.push(`${type} ${name}`));

await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 50));
fs.writeFileSync(path.join(dir, "f.txt"), "x");
await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 300));
fresh.close();
console.log(events.length ? events.join(", ") : "no events");
```

```
$ deno run -A repro.mjs
no events
$ node repro.mjs          # v22.18.0
rename f.txt, change f.txt
```

Deno prints `no events` on every run. It works if the directory is not recreated, or if the new watcher is opened ~100 ms after `close()`.

chokidar does exactly this when a watched directory is deleted and recreated (as `git rebase` can do), so Vite's dev server under Deno stops seeing changes in that directory until restarted.

Possibly related: `FsEventsResource::close` only cancels, and the per-path watch count in `runtime/ops/fs_events.rs` is decremented in `Drop`. `op_fs_events_open` calls `watcher.watch()` only when that count is 0, so a watcher opened before the old resource is dropped would not add a watch for the new directory.

