body_text:
## Problem
On Deno 2.9.7 (first reported on 2.9.5; still reproduces on 2.9.7), aborting a fetch after its `Response` has been collected can leave its streaming HTTP response open. The body reader remains alive and has a pending read, and the supplied `AbortSignal` is aborted, but the server's response stream never receives `cancel()`. Retaining the `Response` through abort makes the same reproduction cancel promptly.

This blocks durable-chat transport disposal tests under parallel load. The transport consumes the response body through an SSE parser without retaining the original live `Response`. Focused runs often pass; forced GC reproduces the lost cancellation without the transport or any dependencies.

## Reproduction
Run the following script with `deno run --allow-net --v8-flags=--expose-gc repro.ts`. It runs once without retaining the response and once retaining it. The timer below bounds this diagnostic; it does not retry a failure.

```ts
const encoder = new TextEncoder();
for (const keepResponse of [false, true]) {
  const cancelled = Promise.withResolvers<void>();
  let serverBody: ReadableStreamDefaultController<Uint8Array> | undefined;
  const server = Deno.serve({ port: 0, onListen() {} }, () =>
    new Response(new ReadableStream<Uint8Array>({
      start(controller) {
        serverBody = controller;
        controller.enqueue(encoder.encode('hello'));
      },
      cancel() { cancelled.resolve(); },
    }))
  );
  const aborter = new AbortController();
  const { ref, reader, retained } = await (async () => {
    const response = await fetch(
      `http://${server.addr.hostname}:${server.addr.port}`,
      { signal: aborter.signal },
    );
    return {
      ref: new WeakRef(response),
      reader: response.body!.getReader(),
      retained: keepResponse ? response : undefined,
    };
  })();
  await reader.read();
  const pending = reader.read();
  void pending.catch(() => undefined);
  await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 0));
  Reflect.get(globalThis, 'gc')();
  await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 0));
  Reflect.get(globalThis, 'gc')();
  console.log({ keepResponse, responseAlive: !!ref.deref() });
  aborter.abort();
  let timer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | undefined;
  try {
    await Promise.race([
      cancelled.promise,
      new Promise<never>((_, reject) => {
        timer = setTimeout(() => reject(new Error('server never cancelled')), 1000);
      }),
    ]);
    console.log('cancelled');
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(String(error));
    serverBody!.close();
  } finally {
    clearTimeout(timer);
  }
  if (keepResponse) console.log(retained!.status);
  await pending.catch(() => undefined);
  reader.releaseLock();
  await server.shutdown();
}
```

Observed:

```text
{ keepResponse: false, responseAlive: false }
Error: server never cancelled
{ keepResponse: true, responseAlive: true }
cancelled
200
```

Expected: abort cancels the server response in both cases while the response body is actively read, regardless of whether the `Response` wrapper remains reachable.

Environment: Deno 2.9.7 stable (also reproduced on 2.9.5), x86_64 Linux; V8 15.0.245.2-rusty; TypeScript 6.0.3.

Related downstream report: rickylabs/netscript#2317 and PR rickylabs/netscript#2318. Diagnostic logs show the supplied live-fetch signal aborting while server cancellation remains absent. The downstream fixture will retain the response explicitly and keep a bounded event wait while this product behavior is investigated upstream.

