Version: Deno 2.9.4 (aarch64-apple-darwin)

The `dictionary` option of `node:zlib` is silently ignored. Compressing with a dictionary produces byte-for-byte the same output as compressing without one, and inflating ignores the dictionary too, so streams compressed with a preset dictionary by Node (or any zlib) can't be decompressed in Deno. Applies to both raw (`deflateRawSync`/`inflateRawSync`) and zlib-wrapped (`deflateSync`) formats.

```js
import zlib from "node:zlib";
const dictionary = Buffer.from("hello world hello world");
const input = Buffer.from("hello world");
const withDict = zlib.deflateRawSync(input, { dictionary });
const noDict = zlib.deflateRawSync(input);
console.log("with dictionary:", withDict.toString("hex"));
console.log("no dictionary:  ", noDict.toString("hex"));
console.log("identical:", withDict.equals(noDict));

// Output produced by Node for the same input + dictionary:
const fromNode = Buffer.from("43620200", "hex");
try {
  console.log("inflate Node output:", zlib.inflateRawSync(fromNode, { dictionary }).toString());
} catch (e) {
  console.log("inflate Node output threw:", e.message);
}
```

Node v24.18.1:
```
with dictionary: 43620200
no dictionary:   cb48cdc9c95728cf2fca490100
identical: false
inflate Node output: hello world
```

Deno 2.9.4:
```
with dictionary: cb48cdc9c95728cf2fca490100
no dictionary:   cb48cdc9c95728cf2fca490100
identical: true
inflate Node output threw: invalid distance too far back
```

Related: #29638 (closed `node:zlib` tracking issue).

