`GPUBuffer.mapAsync()` never resolves in less than about 11 ms on Windows, even when nothing is queued on the GPU, and `GPUQueue.onSubmittedWorkDone()` takes about 15 ms. For readback-heavy workloads (e.g. running a small neural net once per step and reading the result back) this fixed delay dominates: in our case it was most of the wall time of a single inference loop.

**Repro** (no dependencies; `deno run --unstable-webgpu repro.ts`):

```ts
const adapter = await navigator.gpu.requestAdapter();
const device = await adapter!.requestDevice();
const dst = device.createBuffer({ size: 256, usage: GPUBufferUsage.MAP_READ | GPUBufferUsage.COPY_DST });
const t: number[] = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 200; i++) {
  const s = performance.now();
  await dst.mapAsync(GPUMapMode.READ); // nothing is queued on the GPU
  t.push(performance.now() - s);
  dst.unmap();
}
t.sort((a, b) => a - b);
console.log(`mapAsync p50 ${t[100].toFixed(2)} ms`);
```

Deno 2.9.0, Windows 11, RTX 4090 (p50 of 200 calls, two runs each):

| | stock 2.9.0 | with the fix below |
|---|---|---|
| `mapAsync`, nothing queued | 11.39 / 11.46 ms | 0.01 / 0.01 ms |
| tiny copy + submit + `mapAsync` | 15.53 / 15.52 ms | 0.06 / 0.06 ms |
| empty submit + `onSubmittedWorkDone` | 15.44 / 15.42 ms | 0.04 / 0.04 ms |

**Cause.** In `ext/webgpu/buffer.rs` (`map_async`) and `ext/webgpu/queue.rs` (`on_submitted_work_done`) the polling future is:

```rust
while !*done.borrow() {
  self.instance.device_poll(self.device, wgpu_types::PollType::wait_indefinitely()).unwrap();
  tokio::time::sleep(Duration::from_millis(10)).await;
}
```

`device_poll(wait_indefinitely())` blocks until the queue is idle, and the map / work-done callback fires inside that poll, which sends on the oneshot. But the loop then always sleeps 10 ms before it re-checks `done` (set by the receiver future on the same task), so every call pays at least one full sleep after the GPU is already finished; on Windows the timer rounds that up to about 11-15 ms.

**Suggested fix:** yield instead of sleeping (PR to follow). `wait_indefinitely` already blocks until the work is done, so the sleep is not needed for pacing.

Separately, and not addressed by that fix: the blocking `wait_indefinitely` poll runs on the JS thread inside the op, so the JS thread cannot do other work while the GPU computes. Moving that poll onto a blocking-pool thread let JS keep running in our local tests; that is a larger change, so it is only mentioned here.

This issue was investigated and written with the help of an AI assistant (Claude); the measurements were run on my machine.

