Version: Deno 2.9.8 (also reproduced on 2.9.6 and 2.9.7; x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu)

## Summary

When a `fetch` response body arrives as a chunked stream (no `content-length`) in small HTTP
chunks, each `reader.read()` returns a `Uint8Array` that is a view over its **own fresh 64 KiB
(65,536-byte) `ArrayBuffer`**, whatever the chunk's `value.byteLength`. A consumer that holds the
chunks until the body ends (the common "read everything, then concatenate" pattern) therefore
pins **reads × 64 KiB**, not the body size.

Is this intended? If it is, it would help to document it, because nothing in the Streams API
suggests that keeping a 1 KB chunk keeps 64 KiB alive. If it isn't, returning exact-size
buffers (or slices of a shared pool that are freed with their chunks) would fix it.

## Reproduction (self-contained)

```ts
// deno run -A repro.ts <chunkBytes> <sendContentLength: 0|1>
const CH = Number(Deno.args[0] ?? 1000);
const withLen = Deno.args[1] === "1";
const body = new Uint8Array(4 * 1024 * 1024).fill(0x61);

const server = Deno.serve({ port: 0, onListen: () => {} }, () => {
  let off = 0;
  const stream = new ReadableStream<Uint8Array>({
    pull(c) {
      if (off >= body.length) return c.close();
      c.enqueue(body.subarray(off, off + CH));
      off += CH;
    },
  });
  const headers: Record<string, string> = {};
  if (withLen) headers["content-length"] = String(body.length);
  return new Response(stream, { headers });
});

const res = await fetch(`http://localhost:${server.addr.port}/`);
const reader = res.body!.getReader();
const held: Uint8Array[] = [];
const bufs = new Set<ArrayBufferLike>();
while (true) {
  const { done, value } = await reader.read();
  if (done) break;
  bufs.add(value.buffer);
  held.push(value);
}
let backing = 0;
for (const b of bufs) backing += b.byteLength;
console.log({ reads: held.length, distinctBuffers: bufs.size, backingMiB: backing / 2 ** 20 });
await server.shutdown();
```

Results for the same 4 MiB body:

| Delivery | Reads | Distinct buffers | Backing |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 KB chunks, no `content-length` (`repro.ts 1000 0`) | 4,209 | 4,209 | **263 MiB** |
| 1 KB chunks, with `content-length` (`repro.ts 1000 1`) | 71 | 71 | 4.4 MiB |
| 64 KiB chunks, no `content-length` (`repro.ts 65536 0`) | 78 | 78 | 4.9 MiB |

Every buffer in that column measures exactly 65,536 bytes, so the backing total is just
reads × 64 KiB. The amplification only shows up with chunked bodies sent in sub-64 KiB pieces:
with a `content-length` or large chunks, reads are coalesced to about 64 KiB and little is
wasted, so testing with either of those won't reproduce it.

## Where it comes from

The response body is a resource-backed byte stream. `createResponseBodyStream()` calls
`readableStreamForRid(responseBodyRid)` (`ext/fetch/26_fetch.js`, line 367 at v2.9.8), and that helper
builds a `type: "bytes"` stream with `autoAllocateChunkSize: DEFAULT_CHUNK_SIZE`, where
`DEFAULT_CHUNK_SIZE = 64 * 1024` (`ext/web/06_streams.js`, lines 1159 and 1275 at v2.9.8). Its `pull()`
reads into the auto-allocated view (`core.read(rid, controller.byobRequest.view)`) and then
`respond(bytesRead)`s. A chunk smaller than the auto-allocated view is what leaves the rest of
that 64 KiB buffer alive for as long as the returned view is held.

This is not fetch-specific — `readableStreamForRid` is the generic rid-backed stream path
(`Deno.FsFile.readable`, `Deno.stdin`, sockets, …). A 5-byte file read pins 64 KiB with no
server involved:

```ts
const path = await Deno.makeTempFile();
await Deno.writeTextFile(path, "hello"); // 5 bytes
const file = await Deno.open(path);
const { value } = await file.readable.getReader().read();
console.log(value.byteLength, value.buffer.byteLength); // 5 65536
```

(Measured on 2.9.7 and 2.9.8: 100-byte file → `chunkBytes: [100]`, one distinct buffer,
`backingBytes: 65536`.)

## Impact

We hit this in production through Supabase Edge Runtime, which inherits the behaviour (it
depends on the upstream `deno_web` crate — `deno_web 0.213.0` today — so a fix here reaches it
on a dependency bump): a 256 MB isolate receiving a 14 MB embeddings response in ~1 KB chunks
was repeatedly killed for exceeding its memory limit. Replaying that response on standalone
Deno, the held chunks pinned ~900 MB of backing buffers. The consumer that held the chunks was
the AI SDK's `readResponseWithSizeLimit`; that side was filed as vercel/ai#23397 and has since been fixed there. Consumers can
work around it by copying each chunk as it arrives, but they have no reason to suspect they
need to.

---

(Analysis, source pointers and measurements here were prepared with AI assistance and then
re-run by hand on 2.9.6, 2.9.7 and 2.9.8; this is a real production incident from a Supabase Edge
Function, not a synthetic sweep.)

