## Feature Request

`Config::with_custom_head` and `Config::with_on_window` each store one value, so every call replaces the previous one, and there are no getters. A library that takes an app's `Config` and needs its own head content or window callback can't add to the app's: whichever is set last wins.

We hit this in `wdio-dioxus`, which connects Dioxus desktop apps to WebdriverIO for end-to-end tests. Its `install(config)` injects a script through the custom head and registers each new window through `on_window`. If the app loads a stylesheet through `with_custom_head`, as its docs suggest for CSS libraries, the stylesheet disappears under test. If the app sets its head after `install`, our script disappears instead.

```rust
let config = Config::new()
    .with_custom_head(r#"<link rel="stylesheet" href="app.css">"#.into())
    .with_custom_head("<script>/* library */</script>".into());
// Only the second head is injected.
```

## Implement Suggestion

Any of these would work for us:

- methods that add instead of replace (e.g. `with_additional_head`, and an `on_window` that keeps a list of callbacks), leaving today's methods as they are;
- making the existing methods additive;
- getters, so a wrapper can combine the values itself.

The first avoids a behaviour change. The same applies to `WindowConfig` on `main`. Happy to send a PR for whichever you prefer.

