body_text:
This example code has four constructors in class `Parent`, from top to bottom:
1. The first one has a warning: `Redundant specification of type arguments <Interface<? super Parent>>`
    - Applying the associated quick fix results in the second constructor, which is an error.
2. The second constructor has an error: `Cannot infer type arguments for Boxes<>`
    - Seeing as this is an error, either the prior warning was wrong, or this error is wrong.
3. The third constructor has no warnings nor errors.  It's the same as the first constructor, but it first declares a local variable before passing it in to `super(...)`, rather than constructing the object within the `super(...)` statement itself.
4. The same as the third constructor, but with inferred type arguments for the local variable, resulting in the same error as the second constructor.
    - So in this situation, inferring type arguments always results in an error, but specifying type arguments only causes a warning sometimes.

Without using a workaround (constructor 3, or `@SuppressWarnings`), it's a paradox.  Specify type arguments and you get a warning.  Don't specify them (`<>`) and you get an error.  The warning says that you don't need them, the error says that you do.
### Code Example
```java
public class Test
{
//Interface
	/**
	 * @param <P> Parent type
	 */
	public static interface Interface<P>
	{
		//Empty
	}

//Box
	/**
	 * @param <C> Child type
	 */
	public static class Box<C>
	{
		//Empty
	}
	public static final class Boxes<C> extends Box<C>
	{
		@SafeVarargs
		public Boxes(@SuppressWarnings("unused") Box<? extends C> ... children)
		{
			//Empty
		}
	}

//Base
	/**
	 * @param <C> Child type
	 * @param <P> Parent type
	 * @param <S> Self type
	 */
	public static abstract class Base<C extends Interface<? super S>, P extends Base<?, ?, ?>, S extends Base<C, P, S>> implements Interface<P>
	{
		public Base()
		{
			//Empty
		}
		public Base(@SuppressWarnings("unused") Box<C> children)
		{
			//Empty
		}
	}
	public static abstract class ChildA<C extends Interface<? super S>, S extends ChildA<C, S>> extends Base<C, Parent, S>
	{
		//Empty
	}
	public static final class ChildB extends Base<Interface<? super ChildB>, Parent, ChildB>
	{
		//Empty
	}
	public static final class ChildC extends Base<Interface<? super ChildC>, Parent, ChildC>
	{
		//Empty
	}
	public static final class Parent extends Base<Interface<? super Parent>, Base<?, ?, ?>, Parent>
	{
		public Parent(Box<ChildA<?, ?>> boxA, Box<ChildB> boxB, Box<ChildC> boxC, @SuppressWarnings("unused") Object unused0)
		{
			super(new Boxes<Interface<? super Parent>>(boxA, boxB, boxC));
			//Warning: "Redundant specification of type arguments <Interface<? super Parent>>"
		}
		public Parent(Box<ChildA<?, ?>> boxA, Box<ChildB> boxB, Box<ChildC> boxC)
		{
			super(new Boxes<>(boxA, boxB, boxC));
			//Error: "Cannot infer type arguments for Boxes<>"
		}
		public Parent(Box<ChildA<?, ?>> boxA, Box<ChildB> boxB, Box<ChildC> boxC, @SuppressWarnings("unused") String unused2)
		{
			final Boxes<Interface<? super Parent>> boxes = new Boxes<Interface<? super Parent>>(boxA, boxB, boxC);
			//No warnings/errors.

			super(boxes);
		}
		public Parent(Box<ChildA<?, ?>> boxA, Box<ChildB> boxB, Box<ChildC> boxC, @SuppressWarnings("unused") Void unused1)
		{
			final Boxes<Interface<? super Parent>> boxes = new Boxes<>(boxA, boxB, boxC);
			//Error: "Cannot infer type arguments for Boxes<>"

			super(boxes);
		}
	}
}
```
I've simplified this code example as much as I can while still reproducing the problem.  There may be a clever way to simplify this further.

**Note**: The varargs constructor for `Boxes` can be replaced like so and the problem will still occur:
```java
public Boxes(Box<? extends C> childA, Box<? extends C> childB, Box<? extends C> childC)
{
	//Empty
}
```
