body_text:
ECJ rejects a cast that javac accepts with an unchecked warning, when the target type uses a type variable with a recursive bound.

```java
import java.util.function.Function;
public class T3 {
    static <T, E extends Enum<E>, R> Object of(Function<? super T, ? extends R> original) {
        return (Function<E, R>) original;
    }
}
```

```
T3.java (at line 4)
	return (Function<E, R>) original;
	       ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
Cannot cast from Function<capture#1-of ? super T,capture#2-of ? extends R> to Function<E,R>
```

Without the bound (`E` instead of `E extends Enum<E>`), ECJ accepts the cast, so the recursive bound is what makes ECJ conclude that the two types are provably distinct. javac only reports `unchecked cast`.

I found it by compiling the JDK 25 sources with ECJ: ``java.base/jdk/internal/lang/stable/StableEnumFunction.java:115`` is one of the few errors ECJ reports on code that javac builds.

### Environment

