body_text:
The return type variable of a generic method is resolved to its bound when the last argument is a method reference to a generic method, instead of being inferred from the target type.

```java
public class T4 {
    static class P {}
    static class W<E> extends P {}
    interface Op<R extends P, A extends P, S> { R apply(A a, S s); }
    static <R extends P, A extends P, S> R convert(A a, S s, Op<R, A, S> op) { return op.apply(a, s); }
    static class V extends P {
        <F> W<F> f(Class<F> c) { return convert(this, c, V::g); }
        <F> W<F> g(Class<F> c) { return null; }
    }
}
```

```
T4.java (at line 7)
	<F> W<F> f(Class<F> c) { return convert(this, c, V::g); }
	                                ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
Type mismatch: cannot convert from T4.P to T4.W<F>
```

If `g` and `f` are not generic (`W<String> f(Class<String> c)` and `W<String> g(Class<String> c)`), ECJ accepts the code. `V::g` is an inexact method reference, so it is not pertinent to applicability and `R` should be inferred from the target type `W<F>` and from the return type of the reference once `g` is resolved (JLS 18.2.1, 18.5.2.2). ECJ seems to resolve `R` to its bound `P` first.

I found it by compiling the JDK 25 sources with ECJ: ``jdk.incubator.vector/jdk/incubator/vector/AbstractVector.java` (four lines: 206, 743, 756, 768)` is one of the few errors ECJ reports on code that javac builds.

### Environment

- ECJ `org.eclipse.jdt.core.compiler.batch_3.46.100.v20260826-1225` (Eclipse 2026-09), `-21`
- JustJ OpenJDK 25.0.4
- javac 25 accepts the code (`--release 21`)

