body_text:
Found while compiling Guava (`AbstractFutureState`) with ECJ: a private member type is not found when a class-level annotation with an enum attribute and a static import from a subclass are combined.

### Reproducer

Three files in package `p`:

```java
// Anno.java
package p;
public @interface Anno { Level value(); enum Level { FULL } }
```

```java
// AbstractFuture.java
package p;
abstract class AbstractFuture<V> extends AbstractFutureState<V> {
  static final class Listener {}
  static void getDoneValue() {}
}
```

```java
// AbstractFutureState.java
package p;
import static p.AbstractFuture.getDoneValue;
import p.AbstractFuture.Listener;
@Anno(value = Anno.Level.FULL)
abstract class AbstractFutureState<V> {
  boolean cas(Listener e) {
    return H.cas(e);
  }
  private static final AtomicHelper H = null;
  private abstract static class AtomicHelper {
    abstract boolean cas(Listener e);
  }
}
```

### Expected

Compiles, as with javac (`javac --release 8 *.java` and `javac 25`).

### Actual

ECJ 3.46.100 and a current master build, with `--release 8` and `--release 21`:

```
1. ERROR in AbstractFutureState.java (at line 7)
	return H.cas(e);
	       ^
AtomicHelper cannot be resolved to a type
```

Each of the following makes the error disappear: removing the static import (even though it is not used), removing the annotation, or using an annotation whose attribute is an `int` (or a marker annotation, or `@Deprecated`) instead of an enum constant.

The cycle `AbstractFuture extends AbstractFutureState` with `AbstractFutureState` importing from `AbstractFuture` is part of it. My guess (not checked in the compiler code): resolving the enum constant of the annotation forces the resolution of member types while the hierarchy of `AbstractFutureState` is still being connected (triggered by the static import loading `AbstractFuture`), and the member types of `AbstractFutureState` are read before they are complete, so `AtomicHelper` is not found.

With the real Guava sources, the same problem gives 10 errors in `AbstractFutureState.java` (javac compiles it).

I could not find an existing issue.

This report was prepared with the help of Claude (Anthropic); the reproducer was checked with javac and ECJ.

