Found while compiling Guice (`DaggerMethodScanner`, `extensions/dagger-adapter`) with ECJ.

### Reproducer

```java
import java.util.List;
class TypeLiteral<T> {}
class T {
    static <K, V> void m(TypeLiteral<K> k, TypeLiteral<V> v) {}
    void f(List<? extends TypeLiteral<?>> l) {
        m(l.get(0), l.get(1));
    }
}
```

### Expected

Compiles, as with javac 25 (`javac T.java`).

### Actual

ECJ 3.46.100 and a current master build:

```
ERROR in T.java (at line 6)
	m(l.get(0), l.get(1));
	^
The method m(TypeLiteral<K>, TypeLiteral<V>) in the type T is not applicable for the arguments (capture#1-of ? extends TypeLiteral<?>, capture#3-of ? extends TypeLiteral<?>)
```

### What I found

- Two generic parameters are needed: with a single `TypeLiteral<K>` parameter, or `a(TypeLiteral<K> k, Object o)`, ECJ compiles the same arguments.
- The argument has to be a capture variable (`l.get(0)` has type `capture of ? extends TypeLiteral<?>`). With a declared type variable `<C extends TypeLiteral<?>> void g(C c) { m(c, c); }` javac also rejects the call, so ECJ is right in that case.

Possibly related: #3994 (not checked).

This report was prepared with the help of Claude (Anthropic); the reproducer was checked with javac and ECJ.

